\title{Two optimisation waves after the first release, and the level fix}
\taxon{Session}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{performance}
\tag{machine}
\tag{loader}
\tag{levels}
\tag{soundness}
\meta{harness}{Claude Code}
\meta{model-id}{claude-fable-5-2}
\import{base-macros}

\p{The day after [the finalization](ses-A14B). The maintainer had decided not to submit to the arena yet and to go on optimising, with warm wall clock on 8 cores as the metric and gruszka, the fastest checker of the arena, as the mark. The work was done by agents with one lever each, in private copies of the tree, under one rule for everything proposed on top of the default configuration, [the guard](dec-YP0N): on the eight corpora the geometric mean of the warm passes goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, on the executable and on the JVM launcher; and under the gates of the default configuration, judged by official verdicts. An integrator merged each wave, gated and measured the sum, and the orchestrator published it. Branch \code{dev}; commits \commit{c2536e8}, \commit{b8feca1} and \commit{f45b662} (first wave), \commit{55f2db3} (the level fix), \commit{34e8647} (second wave).}

\p{\strong{Where the time went.} [The profile of the release](rep-P11) and its insights said that a warm pass of the executable is bound by the total CPU of the 8 cores, that the native image needs a quarter more CPU than the JVM launcher in the workers' own code, that the loader thread is right behind the workers, and that a tick of the machine gets dearer as a session grows.}

\p{\strong{The first wave}, in the night to 2026-11-25: a hand-kept list of methods that the image inlines by name ([rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A), [ins-CGPT](ins-CGPT)); a frozen level base that the workers share instead of copying it at every publication ([rep-OPT-C](rep-OPT-C), [ins-VLN9](ins-VLN9)); loops without a call in the reader, the loader and the block writer ([rep-OPT-B](rep-OPT-B), [ins-BGII](ins-BGII)). Together, on a quiet host: the executable 8.7 \% faster in the geometric mean of the eight warm passes, every corpus faster, Mathlib 14.57 to 13.11 s with 3 GiB less resident memory; the JVM launcher 3.2 \% faster. The fast and slow splits of rep-OPT-A stayed out. [The integration](rep-INT8), [the decision](dec-JT59).}

\p{\strong{The level fix.} A debug mode of the machine's gate, run at a session cap of 16 MiB for the second wave, declined one declaration of Init with an internal error of the level store, in the pristine tree as in the candidate. [The investigation](rep-INV-1) found a defect older than both waves: the instantiation caches of the level store kept an entry across a level reset when its ids were below the base bounds, and a base list id is not a list of base levels, because one store hash-conses level lists together with name lists ([ins-DWCM](ins-DWCM), [ins-6S1P](ins-6S1P)). Counted in an instrumented build, later sessions were handed another level list than the instantiation, or an id that did not exist, in 8 of 188 runs; every run accepted its corpus, and no wrong verdict was observed or constructed. The fix makes an entry survive a reset only by content; it was published before the second wave was merged. [The changelog entry](log-MVGK); [universe levels](design-universe-levels). The structural repair, separate stores for the two kinds of lists, is open, see [what is left open](prob-HC9J).}

\p{\strong{The second wave}, in the day of 2026-11-25, seven packages. Adopted: a young table of fixed size in front of an old one that grows for each of the machine's three session tables ([rep-OPT-D](rep-OPT-D), [ins-ZKG1](ins-ZKG1), [ins-58I0](ins-58I0)); a second pass over the loader thread, with stretches of three kinds of expression lines and a small table in front of the name index ([rep-OPT-F](rep-OPT-F), [ins-2B4K](ins-2B4K), [ins-T58L](ins-T58L)); a default budget of 2^17 ticks for a probe of eager reduction, and a declaration that repeats an accepted one word for word is admitted and not checked again ([rep-OPT-I](rep-OPT-I), [ins-N4ES](ins-N4ES), [ins-FS8W](ins-FS8W)); the one flag that kept the JVM launcher from starting on a stock OpenJDK ([rep-OPT-J](rep-OPT-J)); and the 78 hostile exports that were written against the closed-pair table, as rows of the machine's hostile suite. Not adopted: the closed-pair table itself ([rep-OPT-H](rep-OPT-H), [ins-BFT4](ins-BFT4), [ins-NCAZ](ins-NCAZ)), huge-page advice for the executable's heap ([rep-OPT-G](rep-OPT-G), [ins-EQAW](ins-EQAW), [ins-SSWJ](ins-SSWJ)), a larger session cap. One package was research only: RLVM against gruszka unit by unit ([rep-OPT-E](rep-OPT-E), [ins-OMW4](ins-OMW4), [ins-RQPE](ins-RQPE)).}

\p{\strong{The integration of the second wave.} [The report](rep-INT9): the merge (two packages meet in one source file, the loader), the open point of the session tables settled in a window of its own, the gates of the merged build before and after the publication, the counters of a deterministic run attributed change by change, the guard of the published build with gruszka in the same windows, the benchmark rows and the optional build. [The decision](dec-RHRE) says what is in the default build and what is not, and records the one verdict the wave changes: a hostile file of the tolerated kind that is accepted since the repeat skip.}

\p{\strong{Recorded from this phase.} New: the decisions [dec-JT59](dec-JT59) and [dec-RHRE](dec-RHRE); the reports named above and [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks), regenerated twice; the changelog entries of the first wave, [log-E7BA](log-E7BA), [log-ZMAE](log-ZMAE) and [log-R7NE](log-R7NE), of the level fix, [log-MVGK](log-MVGK), and of the second wave, [log-5HTT](log-5HTT), [log-I3WM](log-I3WM), [log-0H34](log-0H34), [log-YGJO](log-YGJO), [log-7WDX](log-7WDX) and [log-T6DI](log-T6DI); the insight [ins-TWRM](ins-TWRM) on comparing native images. Revised for the state after the second wave: [the switches](design-switches), [eager reduction](design-eager-reduction), [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions), [the cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ), [the driver](design-driver), [the verdict protocol](design-verdict-protocol), [the export loader](design-export-loader), [official divergence](design-official-divergence), [accepted risks](accepted-risks), [the launchers](design-launchers), [packaging](design-packaging), [universe levels](design-universe-levels), [what the release left open](prob-HC9J) and [the index](index).}
